 ## Overview
-Specific Material and Geometry Rendering is a family of techniques that define how volumetric data is mapped to optical properties (density, color, emission) and how specific geometric constraints or material compositions are handled during ray traversal [G]. It solves the problem of translating abstract simulation data or procedural definitions into physically plausible or artistically controlled radiance contributions within the volume rendering integral [G]. It sets itself apart from general sampling schemes by focusing on the definition of material properties [5335-material-parameter-setting.md], the handling of complex global geometries like planetary cloud shells [3480-procedural-volumetric-cloud-rendering.md], or the specific compositing of multi-material scalar fields [S2, unofficial].
